course: dictionary
lesson: 12042
title: A little roof: the circumflex
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A circumflex is a small mark shaped like a little roof (^), written over a vowel. When you see one, look at the words around it to find out what language the word comes from. «The French word forêt means forest.» The little roof over the e tells you this word is French, not English.
explain: The circumflex often shows that an s used to follow the vowel. «The French word hôpital means hospital.» The little roof over the ô is a clue, and the word looks a little different from its English partner.
explain: English rarely uses the circumflex. You will mostly meet it in borrowed words such as «crème brûlée» or «rôle». When a word has a little roof, you can guess it came from another language, then check a dictionary to be sure.
fact: A circumflex is a small mark like a little roof (^).
fact: In French it can sit over the vowels â, ê, î, ô and û, as in «forêt» and «hôpital».
fact: «Île» means isle, and its circumflex often shows that an s used to follow the vowel.
fact: As a rare science word, circumflex can also mean bending around something, as in some nerves and blood vessels.
fact: Circumflex can also be an adjective, as in «circumflex accent».
exercise: The little roof (^) over a vowel is called what? | Full stop | *Circumflex | Underline | Hyphen
exercise: «In «hôpital», the ô has a circumflex.» What does the circumflex look like? | *A small roof, like ^ | A little dot | A straight line across the top | A tiny tail curling down
exercise: «Crème brûlée» has a circumflex over the û. Where does this word come from? | An old English word | An Australian word | A Scots word | *A borrowed French word
test: In «forêt», which vowel has the circumflex? | â | î | ô | *ê
test: Which description fits a circumflex best? | A long line under a consonant | *A small roof-shaped mark over a vowel | A full stop | A number
exam: What might the circumflex in an unknown French word tell you? | That the word is a number | *That an s may once have followed the vowel | That it is a question | That it is someone's name
